==Portuguese==
[[Image:Alopekis puppies.jpg|thumb|250px|cachorros (sense 1)]]
[[Image:Bernese Dog 10m..jpg|thumb|250px|cachorro (sense 2)]]

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|pt|es|cachorro||cub}}, metathesized form of {{der|pt|eu|txakur}}, {{m|eu|xakur||puppy}}. Compare also {{cog|co|ghjacaru||hound}}, {{cog|sc|giagaru}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|Brazil}} {{IPA|pt|/kaˈʃo.ʁu/|/kaˈʃo.hu/|/kaˈʃo.ɦʊ/}}
* {{a|Portugal}} {{IPA|pt|/kɐˈʃo.ʁu/}}
* {{hyphenation|pt|ca|chor|ro}}

===Noun===
{{pt-noun|m|s|f=cachorra}}

# {{lb|pt|Portugal}} {{l|en|puppy}} {{gloss|a young dog}}
# {{lb|pt|Brazil|Madeira|colloquial}} {{l|en|dog}} (of any age)
# {{lb|pt|Brazil|derogatory}} A [[promiscuous]] man.
# {{lb|pt|by extension}} An unfaithful man.
# {{short for|pt|cachorro-quente|dot=:}} {{l|en|hot dog}}

====Usage notes====
In Brazil, this is the neutral and colloquial term for "dog", whereas in settings of higher formalities {{m|pt|cão}} is used instead. The feminine {{m|pt|cachorra}} is even more avoided in such settings (in which {{m|pt|cadela|cadela}} is used), but is used more often in everyday language.

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|dog}} {{l|pt|cão}}

